{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013mecxR59Jw9CcdzrDeYnEA","type":"tool_result","content":"4:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n20:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n26:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n31:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n37:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n42:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n49:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n55:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n60:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n64:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n66:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n73:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n79:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n84:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n92:**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The click module (the full engine) keeps the rooms, the walking, the verbs, the inventory and the rules; the game paints the rooms and writes the puzzles.\n97:**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, { zoom: 2 })`, its expression by `rig.update(dt, { expr: 'smile' })`), and the story module's nodes and choices for the script, its flags for the routes. `story.log` is the backlog (`{ who, name, text }`: draw the last lines when the player asks), `story.auto = 2` turns pages by themselves after two seconds, `story.skip = true` types and turns them at once (choices still wait). Save with `game.saves` (`story.state()` in its data).\n99:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (the full engine, after the grid module) keeps the rules; the game draws.\n105:**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n111:**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n115:**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n134:**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n140:**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n147:**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n154:**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n161:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n163:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n165:**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n167:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n172:**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n174:**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n178:**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n183:**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n187:**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n189:**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n191:**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n193:**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n195:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n197:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n199:**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2aa65a8-ee1a-45c4-b8e6-f96cf188e23d","uuid":"af7c1d13-c5c5-460f-951b-70aba1b48c5d","timestamp":"2026-11-03T17:55:35.522Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"4:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n20:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n26:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n31:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n37:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n42:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n49:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n55:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n60:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n64:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n66:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n73:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n79:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n84:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACT