{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DTdMw2k2VrMnArLoFr3rAe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: The Wire (2002-2008) - Keep it as gritty and real as the show\n2\t\n3\tGenre: pick the genre(s) that best matches the source and would ultimately make the best game\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: pick the perspective(s) that best matches the genre/source and would ultimately make the best game\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be include in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff, feel free to modify and expand upon it as needed, the rest of the tech stack choices are yours\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: use the engine native style, feel free to modify and expand upon it as needed\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: use the engine native system, feel free to modify and expand upon it as needed\n18\t\n19\tAudio: Use code written Music and sound effects and Animalese / Simlish” / gibberish voice acting for any \"voice\" work.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319] here you can keep all your dev files. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319].html in root when the goal has been completed. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat it as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Keep testing token efficient and lightweight.\n29\t\n30\t## Goal\n31\t\n32\tBuild an original spiritual successor to The Wire (2002-2008) - Keep it as gritty and real as the show.\n33\t\n34\tScope\n35\t\n36\tCreate a feature-complete demo inspired by The Wire (2002-2008) - Keep it as gritty and real as the show. Capture what makes the source distinctive while modernizing and improving its mechanics, pacing, and UX wherever it benefits the game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice / game engine. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n37\t\n38\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n39\t\n40\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n41\t\n42\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the The Wire (2002-2008) - Keep it as gritty and real as the show.\n43\t\n44\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n45\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n46\t\n47\tCommon Issue (~10%): Gamepad horizontal/vertical input is intermittently inverted. As a quick fix, add toggles in Settings to invert horizontal and vertical axes.\n48\t\n49\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n50\t\n51\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Allow the attract mode run.\n52\t\n53\t## Done\n54\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n55\t\n56\t## Source Information\n57\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n58\t- Date: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\n59\t- Source: the work it's based on\n60\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n61\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n62\t- Art Direction: style used\n63\t- Animation: system used\n64\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n65\t- Online: network features used, or none\n66\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n67\t- Research: research done, or none\n68\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n69\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\n70\t- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n71\t\n72\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n73\t{\n74\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n75\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n76\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n77\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n78\t  \"aiHub\": {\n79\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n80\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n81\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n82\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n83\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n84\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n85\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n86\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n87\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n88\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n89\t  }\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\tTesting Feedback\n93\t\n94\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n95\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n96\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n97\t- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n98\t\n99\tEngine Feedback\n100\t\n101\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n102\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n103\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n104\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n105\t- If you had to remove or overhaul one thing from engine, what woudl that be?\n106\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n107\t\n108\tPrompt Feedback \n109\t\n110\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n111\t- What was confusing? \n112\t- What was the hardest thing to do? \n113\t- If there was one test you could of run, what would that test be?\n114\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XnCMPJrdKsvgzZ1BF12Jst","agent_id":"af67e84b720105c7c","session_id":"e2aa65a8-ee1a-45c4-b8e6-f96cf188e23d","uuid":"4b45dda2-1f2e-4b93-9938-500b3dbb66e9","timestamp":"2026-11-03T18:50:58.172Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DTdMw2k2VrMnArLoFr3rAe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review game plan for gaps"}
