{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TbwYNu17gfeZEM6GhivpbQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WIRETAP\n2\t\n3\t*\"Somebody's always listening.\"*\n4\t\n5\tAn original crime game inspired by **The Wire (2002-2008)**. It plays both sides of one West Baltimore corner: a police detail that builds a case with cameras, pagers, payphones and paper, and the crew that runs the corner and adapts to every move the detail makes. It is gritty, slow-burning and human. Nobody gets a clean win.\n6\t\n7\t- **Title:** WIRETAP. I brainstormed 50 riffs on \"The Wire\" and numbered them, then a cryptographic RNG (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50)+1)\"`) drew **7**, which was \"Wiretap\". Keep this title. Don't re-roll it.\n8\t- **File stem** (TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]`, written like the other runs, e.g. `Rewired-TheWire`): `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319`\n9\t- **Deliverables, in this folder (`work/`):**\n10\t  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`: the whole game in one file.\n11\t  - `exit-interview.json`, next to the html (shape in §13).\n12\t  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/`: the dev folder with sources, tools and tests (§3.2).\n13\t- **Readers:** whoever builds this from TASK.md and this plan, possibly another AI model working alone. Every system, number, name and piece of content you need is here. Where you have to make a choice yourself, the plan says so.\n14\t- **Shared folder:** other agents share the parent directory. Don't read files outside this `work/` folder, except your own temp dirs and the two GitHub repos named below.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## 0. Contents\n19\t\n20\t1. The game on one page\n21\t2. Genre, perspective and why\n22\t3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n23\t4. Controls and 10-foot UX\n24\t5. Core systems (field, perception, camera, tail, chase, raid)\n25\t6. Investigation systems (board, notebook, cipher, box, paper, wire, brass/heat/legal/case)\n26\t7. The corner (crew side)\n27\t8. Story mode: setting, cast, five episodes, endings\n28\t9. Versus mode and online play (AI Hub)\n29\t10. Attract mode\n30\t11. Audio: music, effects, voices\n31\t12. Art direction and world building\n32\t13. About/Credits screen and the exit interview\n33\t14. Build order and milestones\n34\t15. Risks and de-risking\n35\t16. Testing plan (lightweight)\n36\t17. Definition of done\n37\t- Appendices: A map · B Annex map · C cipher and pages · D wire calls · E interrogation data · F affidavit and judge · G writing guide and key scenes · H music scores · I voice profiles · J data schemas\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 1. The game on one page\n42\t\n43\t**Core loop (police, \"the detail\"):** *Watch → identify → intercept → decode → act → answer to the brass → climb the chain.*\n44\tYou plant yourself near the corner without getting \"made\", photograph the crew mid-deal, put faces, names and numbers on the Board, write an affidavit good enough for a judge, then sit in the wire room deciding which calls you're legally allowed to hear. Every arrest you make for the bosses' statistics warns the crew. Every week you wait, the Major's patience runs lower.\n45\t\n46\t**Counter-loop (street, \"the corner\"):** between episodes you play **Terrence \"Tee\" Mabry**, the crew's lieutenant, for one night shift. You post lookouts, keep the line moving, move the stash, page the re-up through the code, spot the van that wasn't there yesterday and call \"5-0\". How well you play Tee changes how hard Dana's next day is. That is the game's thesis: *the game is rigged both ways.*\n47\t\n48\t**Modes:**\n49\t\n50\t| Mode | Players | What it is |\n51\t|---|---|---|\n52\t| **The Case** (story) | 1 | Five episodes, about 60-75 min: field missions, the Annex hub, the wire room, the box, the judge, plus four corner interludes as Tee and a finale with two paths and computed endings |\n53\t| **The Game** (versus) | 1-4 | Corner vs Detail, 5-minute rounds. The detail hides among AI civilians and photographs deals; the corner makes money and calls out cops (SpyParty-style hidden identity). Bots fill empty seats. Local split screen for 2, online through AI Hub shared-world for up to 4 |\n54\t| **Attract** | 0 | Starts after 10 s idle on title/menus: a looping reel of bot-played vignettes from every system. Any input exits |\n55\t\n56\t**Pillars**\n57\t1. **Real over cool.** No superheroes. Police work is waiting, paper and patience. Violence is rare, sudden and has weight. Failure usually doesn't end the game. It makes things worse.\n58\t2. **Both sides of the corner.** One simulation (the CornerSim, §7), seen from both sides. Choices on one side ripple into the other.\n59\t3. **Institutions are the antagonists.** The Major's statistics, the judge's rules, the boss's count. Meters (Brass, Heat, Legal, Case) make those pressures playable.\n60\t4. **Watch, wait, listen.** The best tools are observation and patience: staying unnoticed, reading roles, hearing a key line in a call about chicken wings.\n61\t5. **Couch and TV first.** DualSense on a TV at 10 ft. Large type, few choices per screen, prompts with the right glyphs.\n62\t\n63\t**What the player can do:** walk, run, sneak, blend in, watch and read roles, photograph, eavesdrop, talk, pay informants, read payphone numbers, sit in a van and shoot through a long lens, tail on foot with partner hand-offs, chase on foot (vault fences, tackle, cuff), search for ditched evidence, dig through trash, raid a stash garage, interrogate, decode pager codes, build the Board, write and defend an affidavit, run the wire room (minimize, spot-check, log, identify voices, decode live pages), make institutional choices, and play the other side as Tee.\n64\t**What the player cannot do:** shoot people (Dana never fires her gun, and that is on purpose), drive freely (travel between places is a car-ride dialogue scene), climb buildings, or break the law without the game noticing (Legal meter).\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. Genre, perspective and why\n69\t\n70\t- **Genre:** *stealth investigation / crime drama*, with a light **management sim** (the corner) and an **asymmetric social-stealth versus** mode. The show is about surveillance, institutions and the corner economy. A shooter or beat-'em-up would betray it. Stealth (don't get made), deduction (Board, cipher, affidavit, voices) and management (the count) are the show's verbs. Catalogue genre: **Stealth** (§13).\n71\t- **Perspective:** **isometric 2.5D** (`view: 'iso'`, the engine's yaw 45 / pitch 30) over a 72×56-cell West Baltimore neighborhood. Iso turns rowhouse blocks into a readable diorama, shows sightlines and distance (what tails and surveillance depend on) and fits the engine's stealth tooling (`E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, `r.sightCone`, `cut` walls, x-ray). The camera turns in 90° steps (L1/R1) to see behind buildings.\n72\t  - Interiors (Annex, wire room, the box, judge's chambers) are small TileMaps. The Annex uses iso. The others use `brawler` (pitch 25) for close, theatrical framing.\n73\t  - Investigation screens (Board, wire room panels, affidavit, notebook) are HTML \"case file\" panels over the pixel world. The engine's own guidance for a modern adaptation is `E.Shell`-style HTML.\n74\t- **Look:** the engine's native retro-modern pixel style (PS1-era 2D), pushed toward grit: sodium-orange streetlight, overcast daylight, rain, boarded vacants, Formstone, phone wires overhead (the visual motif for a game called WIRETAP).\n75\t\n76\t---\n77\t\n78\t## 3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n79\t\n80\t### 3.1 Stack (pin these)\n81\t| Piece | Version | Source | Notes |\n82\t|---|---|---|---|\n83\t| my-3D2dge **full engine** `my-3d2dge.js` | **v0.85.1** (check `E.version`) | `github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` | One file with every module (story, strategy for `E.Clock`, grid, drive). **Never edit it.** Extend from game code |\n84\t| AI Hub session client `session.js` | **1.4.0** | `raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | Paste it **unmodified** as the **first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`** |\n85\t| AI Hub harness | `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | same repo, `lib/hub/` | Test only. Not shipped |\n86\t| Your game code | vanilla JS (ES2020), one IIFE | this plan | No other libraries. No runtime network. No web fonts (system font stacks only) |\n87\t| Build/test | Node ≥ 18, Playwright 1.63 (preinstalled on this machine) | | Engine tools: `tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs` |\n88\t\n89\tFetch fresh copies (don't rely on any planner temp dir):\n90\t```sh\n91\tDEV=Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319\n92\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git /tmp/wt-engine\n93\tgit -C /tmp/wt-engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n94\tmkdir -p $DEV/engine $DEV/vendor $DEV/tests/hub && cp -r /tmp/wt-engine/dist/handoff/* $DEV/engine/\n95\tcurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js -o $DEV/vendor/aihub-session.js\n96\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f -o $DEV/tests/hub/$f; done\n97\t```\n98\tRead `engine/API.md` whole first. Then read `engine/AI_GUIDE.md` sections *Standard features*, *Heights*, *Mover*, *Perception*, *Shell*, *Lighting* and *Genre recipes → Third-person stealth*, and `engine/docs/ASSETS.md` before writing shapes. Start from `engine/game/game.js` (the template) and `engine/kits/stealth.js` (guards, Suspicion, E.Shell HUD, layered music).\n99\t\n100\t### 3.2 Dev folder layout\n101\t```\n102\tWiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/\n103\t  engine/                 the handoff package, unmodified (my-3d2dge.js, tools/, docs, kits)\n104\t  vendor/aihub-session.js the hub client 1.4.0, unmodified\n105\t  src/                    game sources, concatenated in name order inside ONE IIFE by the build\n106\t    00-core.js            E alias, constants, rng streams, event bus, registries, STATE, utils\n107\t    01-settings.js        game settings specs, gamepad axis-inversion wrapper, glyph style\n108\t    02-input.js           WIRETAP input preset, Controller classes, prompt/glyph helpers (canvas + DOM)\n109\t    03-audio.js           songs (App. H), sfx defs, ambience beds, Animalese voice synth, phone filter\n110\t    04-ui.js              DOM UI kit (screens, focus navigation, hover focus, legend footer), headless-safe\n111\t    05-world.js           neighborhood TileMap (App. A), wall types, facade decorators, shapes, lights, weather, time-of-day presets\n112\t    06-people.js          cast roster (rigs, colors, voices), civilian generator, custom poses\n113\t    07-agents.js          Agent (Mover + rig + brain), routines, FlowField cache, interactions, barks\n114\t    08-perception.js      \"made\" system: observers × police suspicion, behavior factors, HUD heat\n115\t    09-corner.js          CornerSim (roles, customers, sales, stash, count, shutdown, heat hooks)\n116\t    10-camera.js          Watch mode, reticle, read/observe, photo scoring, polaroid renderer\n117\t    11-tail.js            tail routes, counter-surveillance, distance band, partner hand-off\n118\t    12-chase.js           escape graph, ditch, vault, tackle, cuff, search\n119\t    13-raid.js            raid planning, breach, interior reactions, stash search\n120\t    14-board.js           case model + Board screen\n121\t    15-notebook.js        notebook screen (objectives, map, people, evidence, phones, case)\n122\t    16-cipher.js          keypad ciphers + decoder UI\n123\t    17-box.js             interrogation system (box + kitchen configs)\n124\t    18-paper.js           affidavit builder + judge cross-examination\n125\t    19-wire.js            wire room (call scheduler, playback, minimization, logging, voice ID, pages)\n126\t    20-meters.js          Brass / Heat / Legal / Case rules and consequences, ops choices\n127\t    21-story.js           Director (episode beats), mission runner, E.Story setup, epigraphs, montage, endings\n128\t    30-data-*.js          content as data: people, missions, dialogue nodes, calls, routes, evidence, credits\n129\t    40-scenes.js          boot, title, attract shell, episode card, annex, field, box, chambers, wire, corner, montage, credits, about, versus, results\n130\t    50-versus.js          versus rules, roles, bots, split screen\n131\t    51-net.js             Transport (Local / Hub), host sim loop, snapshots, inputs, finish\n132\t    52-attract.js         attract reel + bot drivers\n133\t    60-debug.js           game.debug, deep links, ?dev=1 overlay, test hooks\n134\t    99-start.js           new E.Game, scenes, boot steps, start (or AI Hub round)\n135\t    ui.css                HTML UI theme (case-file look)\n136\t    credits.json          About/Credits + exit-interview source of truth (§13)\n137\t  tools/build.mjs         bundles the single html + writes exit-interview.json\n138\t  tests/\n139\t    headless.mjs          systems + smoke tests on the engine's fixed-step clock (Node vm)\n140\t    browser.mjs           Playwright: gamepad glyphs/invert, attract, touch-only notice, screenshots\n141\t    hub/                  harness.html, bridge.js, round.js + hub-check.mjs\n142\t  index.html              dev page: engine + src files as separate scripts (fast iteration)\n143\t```\n144\t\n145\t### 3.3 Single-file bundle (`tools/build.mjs`)\n146\tOrder inside `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`:\n147\t1. `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n148\t2. `<script>` + `vendor/aihub-session.js` (byte-identical) + `</script>`\n149\t3. viewport meta (`width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover`), hub tags (§9.6), `<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">`, `<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">`, `<title>WIRETAP</title>`, `<style>` with `ui.css`\n150\t4. `<body>`: boot splash markup (run the engine in Node `vm` at build time and insert `My3D2dge.bootSplash('WIRETAP')`), then `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas><div id=\"ui\"></div>`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XnCMPJrdKsvgzZ1BF12Jst","agent_id":"af67e84b720105c7c","session_id":"e2aa65a8-ee1a-45c4-b8e6-f96cf188e23d","uuid":"2c3763ff-cf79-4072-8572-b46c7cdc3a5e","timestamp":"2026-11-03T18:51:03.366Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TbwYNu17gfeZEM6GhivpbQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review game plan for gaps"}
