{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dp2y7vEEwCLNMpAn1hp5TX","type":"tool_result","content":"1505\t- DANA: \"Who's the hammer for, Ricky?\" ROOK: \"Law. Yer.\"\n1506\t\n1507\t**Pigeon (Ep4 kitchen).** Starts at Trust 20 (+15 if Dana chose the respectful option with Odessa in the cold open), Fear 40, 8 exchanges. The goal is Trust ≥ 70.\n1508\t- **△ Be honest:** cite a Board fact (\"I know you watch from the roof. I know Tee pays you forty a day.\"). Trust +15 if it's known, −5 if not.\n1509\t- **□ Promise:** \"Nobody will know you talked to me.\" Trust +20 and sets `promise`, which the montage remembers.\n1510\t- **○ Push:** \"You want to end up like Corey?\" Fear +20, Trust −10. A second Push → ODESSA: \"That's enough. You can go now, Dana Lynn.\" (ends it)\n1511\t- **✕ Listen:** Trust +8. PIGEON (about his birds): \"Homers always come back. You take 'em fifty miles and they come back. That's the thing about 'em.\"\n1512\t- **Special option** (only if Dana listened past S3-4's window): *\"I heard your call.\"* Trust −40. PIGEON: \"You was listening to me?\"\n1513\t- **Success:** PIGEON: \"Thursdays I carry the bag. Mama B's. The back door.\" → `pigeonTrust`; Pigeon becomes the Path B runner.\n1514\t- **Odessa frames it:** \"He's a good boy. He's fourteen. Those two things don't last long around here.\"\n1515\t\n1516\t## Appendix F: Affidavit and the judge\n1517\t**Probable cause:** §6.5 table. A line needs ≥ 4 points including a direct item.\n1518\t\n1519\t**Halloway's question pool.** He asks 3: the weakest lines first, and always any false necessity tick.\n1520\t| Q | He asks | Correct answer | Wrong answers (strike the line) |\n1521\t|---|---|---|---|\n1522\t| Q1 identity | \"How do you know the man on this payphone is the man in your photograph?\" | \"We photographed him using that phone at [time] on [date].\" (needs an ★Act payphone photo of that person) | \"Our informant says so.\" / \"He's on that corner every day.\" |\n1523\t| Q2 informant | \"Your informant is a drug addict. Why should I believe a word?\" | \"He's corroborated by the pager memory and our photographs.\" (needs both) | \"He's been reliable before.\" (no record) / \"He has no reason to lie.\" |\n1524\t| Q3 digits | \"These are numbers on a pager. How do you know they're phone numbers at all?\" | \"Decoded with the crew's keypad code, they match three payphones on Ridley. A crew member described the code in an interview.\" (needs decodes + B2) | \"It's how they do it.\" |\n1525\t| Q4 necessity | \"Why can't you just keep watching the corner?\" | \"Surveillance shows us hand-to-hands, not who supplies them.\" (always true) · \"Their lookouts made us on [date].\" (true only if Dana was made) | the \"made\" answer when it never happened (strike) |\n1526\t| Q5 undercover | ticked falsely: \"Tell me about the undercover buys.\" / not ticked: \"Why no undercover buys?\" | not ticked: \"We have no undercover officer, and anyone from the neighborhood is known.\" | **ticked falsely = caught lying: denied** |\n1527\t| Q6 search | ticked falsely: same trap / not ticked: \"Why not just search the stash?\" | \"A raid gets one package and tips the organization. It won't identify the supplier.\" | ticked falsely = denied |\n1528\t| Q7 scope | \"Why the pager clone? What do you expect to hear?\" | \"Pages from the lieutenant to his crew and supplier: re-up times, the count.\" (needs codes 11/33 decoded) | \"Whatever they say.\" |\n1529\t| Q8 minimization | \"Who's monitoring, and do they know the rules?\" | \"Detective Pettiford. Every call logged, minimized in the first seconds if it isn't pertinent.\" (always correct) | — |\n1530\t\n1531\t**Halloway's lines:**\n1532\t- \"I've signed wiretaps for twenty-two years, Detective, and I regret eleven of them.\"\n1533\t- \"I read every log. If I find a grandmother's phone call in your transcripts, I'll pull this wire myself.\"\n1534\t- Signing: \"You're up. Thirty days.\" (stamp)\n1535\t\n1536\t## Appendix G: Writing guide, key scenes, endings\n1537\t**Tone:**\n1538\t- Understated and specific. People talk around things and say less than they mean.\n1539\t- Humor is dry and dark.\n1540\t- Cops and crew both have bosses, quotas and rent.\n1541\t- Profanity exists but isn't every line.\n1542\t- No exposition dumps. The player learns by watching.\n1543\t\n1544\t**Do:**\n1545\t- Concrete details: the number 22 bus, the Orioles' bullpen, court dates, overtime, a son who needs shoes.\n1546\t- Institutional logic: stats, clearance, OT, the count, the re-up, the 24-hour report.\n1547\t\n1548\t**Don't:**\n1549\t- No slurs, ever, in any setting.\n1550\t- No quoting the show (not its catchphrases or famous lines).\n1551\t- No glamorized violence.\n1552\t- No caricatured dialect. Write it lightly: \"ain't\", \"y'all\", dropped g's now and then, \"yo\" or \"na'mean\" at most once a scene.\n1553\t\n1554\t**Vocabulary** (characters use it; UI and narration stay plain): re-up, package, testers, hoppers, the count, knockers / jump-out boys, 5-0, throwaway, the box, red ball (a high-profile case), OT, stat sheet, clearance rate, Central Booking, the Western, downtown, ASA, \"up on the wire\", minimize, pertinent, PC, CI, dry cleaning (counter-surveillance), walled-off stop.\n1555\t\n1556\t**Bleep list** (Strong language: Bleeped → \"—\" plus a voice bleep): shit, fuck/fucking, motherfucker, bitch, ass, damn.\n1557\t\n1558\t**Epigraphs:** §8.3-8.7 (all original lines spoken by characters in that episode).\n1559\t\n1560\t**Cold open (1.1):**\n1561\t- DUKES: Detective. Neighbor found him at five, going out for the paper. Nobody heard nothing.\n1562\t- DANA: Nobody heard a man get beat on his own steps.\n1563\t- DUKES: That's what nobody said.\n1564\t- LOU *(kneeling by the sheet)*: Cornelius Felton. Thirty-one years driving the number 22. Retired in March.\n1565\t- DANA: He called the corner in. Three times since August. Name and address every time.\n1566\t- LOU: Then somebody read his name out loud to the wrong person.\n1567\t- DANA: You're Homicide. This is yours.\n1568\t- LOU: It's mine till Friday. Then it's a file. You know the street?\n1569\t- DANA: Grew up three blocks off it.\n1570\t- *(at Odessa's stoop)* DANA: Morning, Miss Odessa.\n1571\t- ODESSA: Dana Lynn. Your mama know you're out here this early?\n1572\t- **Choice:** *\"Did you see anything last night?\"* / *\"I remember your choir, Miss Odessa.\"* The respectful second choice sets `odessaRespect` (+15 Trust in the kitchen) and she adds: \"Then you remember I don't say things twice.\"\n1573\t- ODESSA: I saw the TV and the inside of my eyelids. I got a grandbaby to raise, Dana Lynn.\n1574\t- *(the camera drifts up to Pigeon on the roof, feeding his birds)*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XnCMPJrdKsvgzZ1BF12Jst","agent_id":"af67e84b720105c7c","session_id":"e2aa65a8-ee1a-45c4-b8e6-f96cf188e23d","uuid":"900acacc-6090-44ae-a37f-0c89e0f73699","timestamp":"2026-11-03T18:57:57.624Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Dp2y7vEEwCLNMpAn1hp5TX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review game plan for gaps"}
